window.docContents[243].resolve({"/#connectives":{"contents":"The basic building blocks of logic, such as “and”, “or”, “true”, “false”, and “not”, are called logical connectives.\nEach connective defines what counts as evidence of its truth.\nFor example, to prove a statement “A and B”, one must prove both A and B.\nThis means that evidence for “A and B” is a pair that contains both evidence for A and evidence for B.\nSimilarly, evidence for “A or B” consists of either evidence for A or evidence for B.\n\nIn particular, most of these connectives are defined like datatypes, and they have constructors.\nIf  and  are propositions, then “ and ” (written ) is a proposition.\nEvidence for  consists of the constructor , which has the type .\nReplacing  and  with concrete propositions, it is possible to prove  with .\nOf course, decide is also powerful enough to find this proof:\n\n\n\nSimilarly, “ or ” (written ) has two constructors, because a proof of “ or ” requires only that one of the two underlying propositions be true.\nThere are two constructors: , with type , and , with type .\n\nImplication (if  then ) is represented using functions.\nIn particular, a function that transforms evidence for  into evidence for  is itself evidence that  implies .\nThis is different from the usual description of implication, in which  is shorthand for , but the two formulations are equivalent.\n\nBecause evidence for an “and” is a constructor, it can be used with pattern matching.\nFor instance, a proof that  and  implies  or  is a function that pulls the evidence of  (or of ) out of the evidence for  and , and then uses this evidence to produce evidence of  or :\n\n\n\n* Connective* Lean Syntax* Evidence\n* True* * * False* * No evidence*  and * * *  or * * Either  or *  implies * * A function that transforms evidence of  into evidence of * not * * A function that would transform evidence of  into evidence of \n\nThe decide tactic can prove theorems that use these connectives.\nFor example:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Interlude: Propositions, Proofs, and Indexing","header":"Connectives","id":"/#connectives"},"/#nested-actions":{"contents":"Many of the functions in feline exhibit a repetitive pattern in which an  action's result is given a name, and then used immediately and only once.\nFor instance, in :the pattern occurs for :Similarly,  contains the following snippet:\n\nWhen Lean is compiling a  block, expressions that consist of a left arrow immediately under parentheses are lifted to the nearest enclosing , and their results are bound to a unique name.\nThis unique name replaces the origin of the expression.\nThis means that  can also be written as follows:This version of  avoids introducing names that are used only once, which can greatly simplify a program.\n actions that Lean lifts from a nested expression context are called nested actions.\n\n can be simplified using the same technique:In this case, the local name of  could also have been eliminated using nested actions, but the resulting expression would have been long and complicated.\nEven though it's often good style to use nested actions, it can still sometimes be helpful to name intermediate results.\n\nIt is important to remember, however, that nested actions are only a shorter notation for  actions that occur in a surrounding  block.\nThe side effects that are involved in executing them still occur in the same order, and execution of side effects is not interspersed with the evaluation of expressions.\nTherefore, nested actions cannot be lifted from the branches of an if.\n\nFor an example of where this might be confusing, consider the following helper definitions that return data after announcing to the world that they have been executed:\n\n\n\n\n\nThese definitions are intended to stand in for more complicated  code that might validate user input, read a database, or open a file.\n\nA program that prints  when number A is five, or number B otherwise, might be written as follows:\n\n\n\nThis program would be equivalent to:\n\n\n\nwhich runs  regardless of whether the result of  is equal to .\nTo prevent this confusion, nested actions are not allowed in an if that is not itself a line in the , and the following error message results:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Additional Conveniences","header":"2.5.1. Nested Actions","id":"/#nested-actions"},"/#simultaneous-matching":{"contents":"Pattern-matching expressions, just like pattern-matching definitions, can match on multiple values at once.\nBoth the expressions to be inspected and the patterns that they match against are written with commas between them, similarly to the syntax used for definitions.\nHere is a version of  that uses simultaneous matching:\n\nSimultaneous matching resembles matching on a pair, but there is an important difference.\nLean tracks the connection between the expression being matched and the patterns, and this information is used for purposes that include checking for termination and propagating static type information.\nAs a result, the version of  that matches a pair is rejected by the termination checker, because the connection between  and  is obscured by the intervening pair:Simultaneously matching both lists is accepted:\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Getting to Know Lean\u0009Additional Conveniences","header":"1.8.5. Simultaneous Matching","id":"/#simultaneous-matching"}});